# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The absence of ChainID in the `delegateBySig` function exposes the contract to potential signature replay attacks. Without including the ChainID in the signing process, the signature can be replayed on different chains, leading to unauthorized actions and compromising the security of the contract.

## Proof of Concept
```solidity
function delegateBySig(address delegatee, uint256 nonce, uint256 expiry, uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) public {
    require(block.timestamp <= expiry, "ERC20MultiVotes: signature expired");
    address signer = ecrecover(
        keccak256(
            abi.encodePacked(
                "\x19\x01", DOMAIN_SEPARATOR(), keccak256(abi.encode(DELEGATION_TYPEHASH, delegatee, nonce, expiry))
            )
        ),
        v,
        r,
        s
    );
    require(nonce == nonces[signer]++, "ERC20MultiVotes: invalid nonce");
    require(signer != address(0));
    _delegate(signer, delegatee);
}
```
The following code does not include the ChainID when constructing the message to be signed. This omission allows the signature to be replayed on other chains without validation against the intended ChainID.

By incorporating the chainId parameter and including it in the message that is being signed, the modified code ensures that the signature is specific to a particular chain and prevents signature replay attacks on other chains.

